WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.220
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.240
Hey Crazies. We hit 100,000 subscribers!

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.660
What?!

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.219
I can't believe this many people were like:
Yeah, I’ll watch some more of this guy.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.360
Thank you all so much for watching.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.600
Let celebrate!

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.180
The sheer size of 100,000 is demanding a scale video.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.760
A while back, I did one showing the Earth
and Moon.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.340
At a scale of 52,800,000 to 1,

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.440
the Earth and the Moon where the size of a basketball and tennis ball.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.960
But why stop there?!

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.440
Rule number 4 here in the asylum is
"Go big or go home."

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.940
Let’s take one more step out and do the
entire solar system!

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.240
But, just to keep things interesting, let’s
lay down some ground rules.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:46.980
One!

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.280
We’ll be using the same scale we used in
the Earth/Moon video:

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.160
52,800,000 to 1.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:53.920
Two!

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.020
For each astronomical object, there will also
be a normal-sized object.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.460
Just like the Earth and the Moon where a basketball and a tennis ball,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.840
we’ll be doing the same for all the
other planets and the Sun.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:05.700
Three!

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.280
Both the size of the objects and the distances
between them will be to the same scale.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.100
I’m going to need some adventure clones.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.280
Go go go! Go go! Places to be! Go!

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.540
The natural place to start a solar system
video is with the Sun:

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:43.010
That yellow-white star at the center of our solar system.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:47.790
It contains 99.8% of the solar system’s
mass.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.080
For some perspective, let's say the mass of the solar system is 1000 dollars,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.700
the Sun will be 998 of those dollars, which leaves
only two dollars for everything else.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.800
The Sun is in control.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.100
It’s also really big: 864,337 miles across.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.340
That’s a little over 109 Earths just across its diameter.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.280
In terms of volume, it’s 1.3 million Earths.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.300
That’s big.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.720
Does that mean you have to find an object
that’s 109 basketballs across?

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.880
Yep, and I have just the thing.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.960
Just outside Detroit in Allen Park, Michigan,
there’s giant tire that’s exactly the right size.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.760
Like, an actual tire made of rubber.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.020
It was made for the 1964 New York World's Fair to be Ferris wheel.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.160
If you don’t believe me, here’s a picture from the brochure.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.940
This tire is 85 feet or 26 meters across,
which is the perfect size for our scale:

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.180
109 basketballs across.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.240
Seriously, that’s one of my adventure clones right there.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.620
It’s the perfect launch point for an insane scale model of the solar system.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.840
The closest planet to the Sun is Mercury,
which is about the size of a softball on our scale.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.440
It's pretty small compared to that giant tire
and probably farther away than you’re thinking.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.700
On a map, it’s about 0.68 miles or 1.1 kilometers
from the scaled Sun.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.020
A distance easily walked by one of my adventure clones.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.840
Here you can see the tire without any trouble.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.360
The Sun is still rather large in the sky at this distance.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.739
Mercury’s orbit is pretty eccentric though,
so that 0.68 miles is just an average.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.920
It’s 0.54 miles at closest and 0.82 miles at farthest.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:38.560
That has dramatic effects on what the Sun looks
like in the sky over the course of Mercury’s year.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.680
For comparison, here’s what the Sun looks like from Earth.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.740
Next up, Venus!

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.500
It’s about the size of a soccer ball on our scale.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:52.840
On the map, Venus is about 1.3 miles or
2.1 kilometers away from the scaled Sun,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.100
which passes through a shopping center, known to locals as “The Hill.”

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:02.480
That 1.3 miles is a sizable distance from
the giant tire we’re using as our Sun.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.180
You can’t see the tire anymore, but that’s
only because there’s stuff in the way.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.700
None of that stuff is part of our model though.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.400
In space, we’d have a clear view and could
still see the Sun in the distance.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.720
You might have noticed a soccer ball is only
a little bit smaller than a basketball,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.580
which means Venus is about the same size as
Earth,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.960
so it’s no surprise it’s sometimes called
Earth’s sister planet

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.120
and it’s our closest neighbor, but not this close.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.920
That would be both terrifying and apocalyptic.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.720
AHHHHHHHHHH!!!!!!!!!

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.540
On our scaled map, the Earth and Venus are
actually about half a mile apart.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.640
To scale, these two objects are half a mile apart.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.440
That puts the Earth an average of about 1.8 miles
or 2.9 kilometers from the scaled Sun.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.200
The best location it passes through is Beaumont Hospital in Dearborn.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.800
That looks something like this.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.400
Again, the giant tire is blocked by things like buildings,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.940
but, with a clear view, you could still see
it in the distance.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:05.700
From the surface of the Earth, the Sun only
spans about half a degree in the sky,

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.740
but so does the Moon, which is what makes
total solar eclipses possible.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.920
Speaking of the Moon, I mentioned earlier
that was the size of a tennis ball on our scale,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.840
but not located this close to the Earth.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.880
That tennis ball needs to be 24 feet or
7.3 meters from basketball

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.720
and then orbit around it every 27 days.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.780
Now, for the last of the inner planets: Mars.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.620
It’s about the size of this hamster ball on our scale.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.060
Probably the most obsession-inducing planet in human history.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.690
We’ve sent multiple probes.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.880
We’ve seen faces that weren’t there.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.760
Back in the day, people were convinced there
were canals on the surface.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.920
We’d even like colonize it someday.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.440
It’s in the Goldilocks zone like Earth,
so that’s not out of the question.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.920
Milton can tell you all about it.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.540
(Alien Noises)

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.260
Really?

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:51.760
(Alien Noises)

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.220
Huh. Alright.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:59.400
Mars orbits the scaled Sun at a distance of
2.7 miles or 4.3 kilometers,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.100
which conveniently passes through the parking lot of The Henry Ford Museum.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.060
You can actually tour it in Google Street View.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.460
There’s some pretty cool stuff in there.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.280
You know, if you like museums.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.820
Here’s our scaled Mars at that location.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.620
An average human on smooth ground can see
about 3 miles in any direction,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.980
so the scaled Sun should be just barely on
this side of the horizon.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:25.040
From the surface of Mars, that’s only 0.35 degrees in the sky.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.940
OK, so we’ve reached the edge of what we
call the inner solar system.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.600
These are the inner planets:
Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.800
They’re tiny in comparison to that giant tire we’re using as our Sun

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.740
and they all orbit within 3 miles of the Sun on the map.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:47.200
We define the distance from the Earth to the Sun
to be one Astronomical Unit or AU.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.720
That puts the entire inner solar system well within 2 AU.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.560
The outer planets are a different story all together.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.300
We don’t reach another planet until a little over 5 AU.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.360
This is where Jupiter orbits.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.650
It’s big and it’s massive.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:06.400
You remember, that 1000 dollars that represents
the mass of the solar system?

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.820
The Sun was 998 dollars, leaving
only 2 dollars for everything else.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.640
Jupiter uses up almost an entire dollar
of that 2 dollars we have left.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.780
So it’s really massive, but it’s also big.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.480
Size and mass are two different things.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.620
You can fit almost 11 Earths across
the diameter of Jupiter.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:31.600
That means you can fit over 1300 Earths inside the volume of Jupiter.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.740
It’s pretty much maxed out in size though.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:34.540
Fun Fact!

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:39.140
If you added more mass to it, it wouldn’t
actually get any bigger, just denser.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:44.180
If we scale that diameter down, Jupiter is 8.7 feet across.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.460
That’s 104 inches or 265 centimeters!

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:50.040
What did you find that big?!

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:54.960
Well, I didn’t, but just imagine a
giant round conference table.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:01.680
On the map, Jupiter’s orbit is almost 9.2 miles
or 14.7 kilometers from the Sun

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.300
and passes right through Wayne State University
in downtown Detroit.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.980
Home to a lot of really cool architecture and
right next to the Detroit Institute of Arts.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.560
That big conference table looks something like this.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.780
As usual, we’re including an Adventure Clone for scale.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:19.820
In space, you can see the Sun in the distance
because there’s nothing in the way.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:23.640
I mean, humans eyes can still see the Sun out to about 60 light years,

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.840
but we have a problem with our scale model.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:32.140
At 9 miles, the giant tire we’re using for
a scaled Sun is below the horizon.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.360
The Earth’s curvature has moved it
out of our line of sight.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.120
Next stop, Saturn!

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:43.420
On the map, Saturn orbits at over 16.8 miles
or 27 kilometers.

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:48.060
That’s 10 AU from the Sun, twice the orbit
of Jupiter!

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:52.000
At this distance, Saturn’s orbit passes
through Willow Run Airport,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.060
where you’ll find the Yankee Air Museum.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:57.680
They’ve got a bunch of old planes, and air force trinkets, and stuff.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.720
As you can see Adventure Clone had a really good time.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.140
Did you decide to make this crazy scale model of the solar system

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.920
just so you could send an Adventure Clone to all these cool places?

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.200
Maybe?

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.540
Anyway, Saturn is pretty big on our scale
too, about 7.2 feet or 220 centimeters.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.840
That’s a much more reasonably-sized conference
table, but still big.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:19.380
Although, its rings make it look even bigger than it is.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.360
Here’s our reasonably-sized conference table
known as Saturn.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.920
That’s just the planet though.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.520
Its rings stretch out to about here.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.660
You can fit over 9 Earths across its diameter,
even more across the rings.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.960
But in space, Saturn is pretty lonely.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.880
When the Sun expands into a red giant, we
should just move the Earth there.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.300
We can orbit Saturn and have a view like this every day.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:43.999
That’d be pretty cool, if you ask me.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.580
The next planet out is Uranus, which would
be about the size of this hula-hoop.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.620
It’s still pretty big, about 38 inches or
96 centimeters in diameter,

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.780
but at least I have a real object this time.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:59.540
That puts its orbit out at 33.8 miles or
54.4 kilometers.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:04.340
That’s almost 20 AU from the Sun, almost
twice the orbit of Saturn,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.640
which sends its orbit right by the Imagination Station in Brighton.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.660
It’s the coolest playground set up I’ve ever seen.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.200
This thing is huge.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.320
It’s right next to a big pond too.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.580
You know, if you like nature, but not too much nature.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.720
You can fit about 4 Earths across its diameter.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:24.220
It’s no wonder they call these outer planets "giants."

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:28.260
Its actual appearance is pretty plain though,
not a lot of cloud detail.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.880
Some might even call it the most boring planet,

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.820
but I think it’s pretty cool that it rotates on its side.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:37.280
That’s an axial tilt of 98 degrees!

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.220
There must be some weird seasons.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.860
Last, but certainly not least, Neptune.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:46.180
You can see it's only a little smaller than Uranus,
but they’re really far apart.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:52.300
Neptune is located a whopping 53 miles or
85 kilometers from the scaled Sun.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:57.420
That’s about 30 AU, another 10 AU beyond Uranus!

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.200
They’re not kidding when they call these outer planets.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:04.680
On the map, it passes right through the Lost Railway Museum in Grass Lake.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.700
Another museum?

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.140
Yeah, I like museums, OK?

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:13.040
Anyway, the museum is on the edge of downtown
Grass Lake right on Michigan Ave.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.200
It’s full of old rail-cars and stuff.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:19.440
Out back there are railroad tracks, which
is a perfect location for an adventure clone

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.460
to hold the tube. Uh, I mean Neptune.

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:27.680
It often has a dark storm with winds approaching
1500 miles per hour.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.320
That’s the fastest winds in the solar system.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.980
OK, so we’ve reached the edge of what we call
the outer planets.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:39.100
Here they are in all their glory:
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:43.500
They’re pretty big compared to the inner
planets, but still small next to the Sun.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.920
Remember, our scaled Sun is the size of this giant tire.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:51.280
It’s clear from the sizes of their orbits
why we split the solar system in two,

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:54.660
but there’s even a physical division called
the Asteroid Belt.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:59.380
That's not the only place we find asteroids,
just an interesting concentration of them.

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:02.829
We find rocks of many sizes all over the solar system.

00:12:02.829 --> 00:12:06.340
Most of them are comets in the Kuiper Belt,
a belt that includes Pluto,

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:10.100
and the Oort Cloud, which stretches out so
far beyond the planets

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.120
it’s difficult to even imagine.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.760
This is the limit of the Sun’s control.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:20.720
Any object beyond that is closer to nearby stars like
Alpha or Beta Centauri than it is to the Sun.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.520
This is probably the biggest scale we can
use for the solar system

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:27.400
and still have the sizes of the objects reasonable.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.220
So, was that a crazy enough solar system model for you?

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:31.580
Let us know in the comments.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.020
And again, thank you all for continuing to watch my crazy face.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.700
The last couple of years have been quite a trip.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.920
Also, thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.300
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:45.680
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:50.380
If you want to help support my channel,
check out my Patreon if you haven't already.

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:53.520
There’s also a new YouTube feature called
"channel memberships."

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:55.860
You just click this join button to sign up.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:56.980
I’d still prefer Patreon.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.840
They have way more features and they take a much smaller cut,

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.600
but for those of you who don't like it for whatever reason, here's your alternative.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.220
Anyway, thanks for watching!

